#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:5
msgid "Scheduler preemption"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:7
msgid ""
"The kernel can be built to run kernel code either uninterruptibly, or with "
"varying degrees of preemptibility.  These are the *preemption models*. They "
"apply to kernel code only: a task running in user space is always preempted "
"by the scheduler, whichever model is selected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:12
msgid ""
"When CONFIG_PREEMPT_DYNAMIC is enabled the preemption model can additionally "
"be selected at boot time with the ``preempt=`` command line parameter, "
"without rebuilding the kernel.  See Documentation/admin-guide/kernel-"
"parameters.txt for the parameter itself. The active model can also be read "
"and changed through /sys/kernel/debug/sched/preempt, which exists for "
"debugging purposes and may change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:20
msgid "The models selectable at runtime are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:23
msgid "none"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:23
msgid ""
"No preemption of kernel code other than at explicit ``cond_resched()`` / "
"blocking points."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:25
msgid "voluntary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:25
msgid "As ``none``, plus ``might_sleep()`` sites."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:26
msgid "full"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:26
msgid ""
"Any section that is not explicitly preempt disabled may be preempted at any "
"time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:28
msgid "lazy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:28
msgid ""
"As ``full``, except that the scheduling request is delayed until the return "
"to user space or the next tick, whichever comes first.  This delay does not "
"apply to real-time tasks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:34
msgid ""
"Not every model is available on every kernel.  An architecture that provides "
"lazy-preempt support offers only ``full`` and ``lazy``, and ``none`` and "
"``voluntary`` are rejected there.  A PREEMPT_RT kernel always offers "
"``full``, and ``lazy`` as well if the architecture provides it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:39
msgid "The model that is actually active is reported in the boot log::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:44
msgid "Honouring a scheduling request"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:46
msgid ""
"A wakeup makes a task runnable, and the scheduler then decides whether it "
"should run.  If there is an idle CPU which is suitable then the task is "
"moved there.  Otherwise the scheduler has to decide which task to preempt, "
"and the task that has to leave the CPU gets a reschedule flag.  In the "
"tracing output this is the ``need-resched`` column, ``N`` for "
"TIF_NEED_RESCHED and ``l`` for TIF_NEED_RESCHED_LAZY."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:53
msgid ""
"The kernel then has to honour that request.  A task executing in user space "
"can always be preempted; a task executing in kernel space can only be "
"preempted where it is safe to do so, and how that is decided is what "
"separates the models:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:58
msgid "``none``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:59
msgid ""
"Code paths with long loops contain explicit ``cond_resched()`` calls, which "
"perform the scheduling."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:62
msgid "``voluntary``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:63
msgid ""
"As ``none``, and functions which are known to be able to block gain a "
"``cond_resched()``-style scheduling point as well, through ``might_sleep()``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:67
msgid "``full``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:68
msgid ""
"Explicit preemption points are no longer involved.  The kernel tracks "
"whether it may be preempted and schedules once the request can be honoured."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:72
msgid "``lazy``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:73
msgid ""
"As ``full``, with one difference: the request the fair scheduler sets is "
"TIF_NEED_RESCHED_LAZY, which is not honoured immediately even where it could "
"be.  It is delayed until the task returns to user space, so that the in-"
"kernel work runs to completion and every lock has been dropped before the "
"CPU is given up.  If the task has not scheduled on its own by then, the "
"request is turned into a full TIF_NEED_RESCHED on the next HZ tick; this is "
"visible as ``B`` in the ``need-resched`` column."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../scheduler/sched-preemption.rst:82
msgid ""
"Real-time tasks always use TIF_NEED_RESCHED, so the delay above does not "
"apply to them.  ``lazy`` is therefore not a replacement for PREEMPT_RT: it "
"keeps most of the responsiveness of ``full`` for SCHED_NORMAL tasks while "
"letting them run to completion, which avoids leaving a lock behind that "
"another task will immediately ask for, and avoids pushing cache-hot data out "
"that would have to be pulled back in."
msgstr ""
